# PostgreSQL Container Shared Memory (`/dev/shm`)

## What the error means

- Message like **`could not resize shared memory segment "/PostgreSQL.<digits>"`** with **`No space left on device`** refers to the **container’s tmpfs `/dev/shm`**, not necessarily host disk space on `/var/lib/postgresql/data`.
- PostgreSQL uses shared memory segments backed by **`shm_size`** on the **db** service in Compose.

## What to do

- Increase **`shm_size`** on the Timescale/Postgres service (e.g. in `docker-compose.yaml` under `db:` / dual-run `db_pg18:`) until heavy queries and parallel workers stop failing. Canonical compose today sets **`shm_size: "16gb"`** with **`shared_buffers=64GB`** on the `db` service (and the same pair on **`db_pg18`**, which becomes production `db` at cutover) — keep **`docs/reference/compose-services.md`** (`db`, `db_pg18`) and **`docs/how-to/operate/migrate-to-postgresql-18.md`** (dual-run **`db_pg18`**) aligned when changing either. Smaller dev hosts may need a lower pair; document the tradeoff rather than silently shrinking production defaults.
- After changing **`shm_size`**, recreate the db container so the limit applies. Dual-run **`db_pg18`** recreate and migrate steps live in **`docs/how-to/operate/migrate-to-postgresql-18.md`** (not the install tutorial).

